Pages = {416-425},
Note = {9th Biennual Conference of the
   International-Society-for-Theoretical-Psychology, Calgary, CANADA, JUN
   03-08, 2001},
Organization = {Int Soc Theoret Psychol},
Abstract = {Recent advertisements for evolutionary psychology propose adaptationist
   explanations of human cognition, emotion, and social behavior in terms
   of natural selection. It will be argued that such Darwin-speak should be
   handled with care. First, it is Cosmides and Tooby's aim to integrate
   psychology within the laws of the hard science of biology. However, they
   seem to have missed the fact that biology, especially evolutionary
   biology, is itself not integrated, and is in many respects hardly
   comparable with a science like physics. Evolutionary biology is
   characterized by the locality, contingency, historicity, and
   heterogeneity of its explanations. Furthermore, evolutionary
   explanations require historical evidence, and the hypothesis that
   current mental capacities were selected in a Stone Age environment,
   requires more evidence than is likely to be forthcoming. Redesigning
   psychology with an eye to its integration in biology, as Cosmides and
   Tooby propose, is therefore not a good idea. However, evolutionary
   hypotheses may be valuable as heuristics, and as an additional
   perspective in psychology.},
