Year = {1998},
Volume = {2},
Pages = {733-736},
Note = {XIIIth Annual International Occupational Ergonomics and Safety
   Conference, YPSILANTI, MI, JUN 11-14, 1998},
Abstract = {Wavelet transforms were studied to er;tract features from the human EEG
   with which visual emotion call be discriminated. EEC signals from seven
   subjects were analyzed. Multi-resolution analysis was used for
   performing discrete wavelet analysis. Hence, the original signal was
   decomposed into ``approximation{''} versions and ``detail{''} versions
   until 6 levels by using discrete wavelet analysis. However, only 6
   detail versions were studied to classify EEG signals evoked by visual
   stimulus. The changes of detail versions increased from high frequency
   DI to low frequency D6. In D3 and D4, there were more significant
   changes in occipital than frontal. Ones in D5 and D6 partially did more
   variation in frontal than occipital. Laterality was shown that tight
   hemisphere showed more changes than left. Consequently, there were
   significant differences between two (positive and negative) EEG signals
   in respect to wavelet transform. So, features based on representations
   related on details can be used to classify or distinguish emotional EEG
   signals.},
