   (ICEST 2011), Singapore, SINGAPORE, FEB 26-28, 2011},
Organization = {Asia Pacific Chem Biol \& Environm Engn Soc; Int Assoc Comp Sci \&
   Informat Technol},
Abstract = {CDM (Climatological Dispersion Model) is a classical air pollution model
   based on the Gaussian approximation and aimed at computing the long term
   (monthly and annual) averages of air pollution concentration in an urban
   area. The concentration at any number- of receptors is computed as the
   result of up to 2500 aerial and 200 point sources on flat terrain. It
   can compute two pollutants at the same time and has been used for both
   SO2 and NOX. In order to predict the amount and dispersion rate of major
   air pollutants (CO, CO2, NOx and Sox) in the studied area, we use CDM
   during two different cold and warm seasons in four daily periods of
   time. Necessary modeling data were classified in two categories includes
   sources of dispersion such as diameter and height of stacks and
   climatological data such as temperature, wind speed and direction.
   Finally we confirmed our results with field sampling and Tecplot
   software.},
