   (ICSC), Rome, ITALY, SEP 07-11, 2015},
Organization = {Dept Psychol, Sapienza Univ Rome; Catholic Univ Leuven; Cassianeum Fdn;
   Catholic Univ Eichstatt Ingolstadt; LMU Munich; Dept Psychol, Peking
   Univ},
Abstract = {Situated cognition seems incompatible with strong decoupling, where
   representations are deployed in the absence of their targets and are not
   oriented toward physical action. Yet, in art consumption, the epitome of
   a strongly decoupled cognitive process, the artwork is a physical part
   of the environment and partly controls the perception of its target by
   the audience, leading to immersion. Hence, art consumption combines
   strong decoupling with situated cognition.},
