Note = {7th International-Water-Association (IWA) Regional Membrane Technology
   Conference (IWA-RMTC), Palermo, ITALY, JUN 18-21, 2024},
Organization = {Int Water Assoc},
Abstract = {This study investigated the transformation potential of a three-stage
   anoxic/oxic (A/O) moving bed biofilm reactor (MBBR) system for the
   removal of various pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs),
   along with the composition of its microbial community in
   carrier-attached biofilms. To achieve this, pilot-scale batch
   experiments were conducted within each reactor. For batch experiments,
   HPLC-MS/MSwere employed to reveal the elimination of 10 PPCPs, and qPCR
   were applied to identify the composition of the microbial community. The
   results indicated that all ten selected PPCPs were detected with
   influent concentrations ranging from 81.5-23037.6 ng/L. The established
   A/O-MBBR system showed a significant removal effect on all of PPCPs.
   However, there was considerable alteration in bacterial genera between
   autumn and winter. This research elucidated the distribution and removal
   efficiency of PPCPs in the system, targeted to provide the necessary
   theoretical basis for optimizing urban wastewater treatment processes
   containing PPCPs and reducing pollutant levels.},
