Organization = {KTH Royal Inst Technol; Univ So Queensland; KWR Watercycle Res Inst;
   Univ Newcastle; Int Soc Groundwater Sustainable Dev; Inst Urban
   Environm; China Univ Geosciences; Univ S Australia, CRC CARE; China
   Assoc Sci \& Technol, China Ctr Int Sci \& Technol Exchange; OPCW},
Abstract = {The surface of ferrihydrite adsorbs arsenic (As) effectively. In this
   investigation, the As laced water samples collected from Geita and Mara
   regions within the Lake Victoria Basin (LVB) under DAFWAT project were
   simulated on Visual MINTEQ 3.1 software to determine the amount of
   ferrihydrite required to adsorb a given amount of As from water. Model
   simulations show that As concentration of <= 1 mM can be completely
   adsorbed by 4 g L-1 ferrihydrite. Previous studies show that the lower
   pH 4 to 4.5 influences adsorption, while it decreases as pH increases as
   well as when As concentration increases. The increase of adsorbent dose
   to 4 g L-1 has shown to improve As(V) adsorption on pH 5 to 8 at 100\%.
   The amount of adsorbent can now be used for laboratory adsorption
   experiments by using iron-based materials or commercial ferrihydrite.},
